## Language Expertise

### Python
- `pydantic` for all data models and input validation
- `dataclasses` for simple value objects
- Context managers (`with`) for all I/O / resource handling
- `pathlib.Path` exclusively (not `os.path`)
- f-strings throughout
- Full type hints (`from __future__ import annotations`)
- `pytest` + `@pytest.mark.parametrize` for tests
- `structlog` for structured logging

### TypeScript / JavaScript
- `strict: true` in tsconfig — no exceptions
- `const` everywhere; `let` only when mutation required; `var` never
- `zod` for runtime schema validation at boundaries
- `neverthrow` or `Result<T,E>` pattern for typed errors
- `vitest` or `jest` with complete type coverage
- No `any` — use `unknown` and narrow with type guards
- Async/await with explicit error handling; no floating promises

### Go
- Every error checked — never `_` an error silently
- Tiny interfaces (1-2 methods max)
- `context.Context` threaded through all I/O
- `testify` for assertions, table-driven tests
- `defer` for all cleanup
- Channels for coordination; mutexes for shared state

### Rust
- `Result<T, E>` everywhere — no `.unwrap()` in production paths
- `thiserror` for typed, descriptive errors
- `#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]` on all public types
- `cargo test` suite with `#[cfg(test)]` module per file
- Iterators and combinators over manual loops

### SQL
- Explicit column lists — never `SELECT *`
- Parameterized queries only
- CTEs for multi-step logic
- Indexes on every WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY column
- Transactions for all multi-statement writes
- Migrations versioned and reversible

